$DIR/main.p:18:22: pp.a = ... uses the per-CPU variable pp outside a region where the task cannot move to another processor
    pp has one copy for each processor. This is an access to part of the
    variable, so the task could move to another processor in the middle of it
    and read one processor's copy and write another's. It must be inside a
    region where the task cannot move: preemption disabled (rt.DisablePreemption
    to rt.EnablePreemption), maskable interrupts disabled, or an rt.Lock held. A
    lone load or store of a whole variable of at most a machine word is allowed
    anywhere.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the access inside a region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        // the access to pp
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:2: the call of set uses the per-CPU variable pp outside a region where the task cannot move to another processor
    pp has one copy for each processor. This is a call of set, which stores pp,
    from a value or under a condition that depends on the load of pp at
    main.p:22, so the task could move to another processor in the middle of it
    and read one processor's copy and write another's. It must be inside a
    region where the task cannot move: preemption disabled (rt.DisablePreemption
    to rt.EnablePreemption), maskable interrupts disabled, or an rt.Lock held. A
    lone load or store of a whole variable of at most a machine word is allowed
    anywhere, as long as the store does not depend on a load of the same
    variable.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the load and the store inside one region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        x := pp
        set(x)
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)
